Title: A Chameleon
Credit: Adapted from the story by
Author: Anton Chekhov
Source: Translated by Constance Garnett. Accolade's sample script.
Draft date: November 2026

EXT. MARKET SQUARE - DAY

{{1}}

Noon heat. Not a soul in the square. The open doors of the shops and taverns gape like hungry mouths. Not even a beggar near them.

The police superintendent OTCHUMYELOV (50s) crosses in a new overcoat, a parcel under his arm. YELDYRIN (30s), a red-haired policeman, strides after him with a sieve full of confiscated gooseberries.

VOICE (O.S.)
So you bite, you damned brute? Lads, don't let him go! Biting is prohibited nowadays! Hold him! {{2}}

EXT. PITCHUGIN'S TIMBER-YARD - DAY

{{3}}

A white borzoy puppy hops out of the yard on three legs, yelping. HRYUKIN (40s), the goldsmith, starched cotton shirt, waistcoat unbuttoned, runs after her, throws himself forward and seizes her by the hind legs.

HRYUKIN
Don't let go!

INT. TAVERN - DAY

Sleepy drinkers lift their heads at the noise. One of them pushes his chair back and goes to the door to look.

EXT. MARKET SQUARE - DAY

YELDYRIN
It looks like a row, your honour... {{4}}

{{5}}

Otchumyelov makes a half turn to the left and strides towards the crowd.

EXT. PITCHUGIN'S TIMBER-YARD - DAY

{{6}}

A crowd has sprung out of the earth around the gate. Hryukin holds his right hand in the air and shows a bleeding finger to everyone. The puppy sits in the middle of the crowd, fore-paws out, trembling all over.

OTCHUMYELOV
What's it all about? What are you here for? Why are you waving your finger? Who was it shouted? {{7}}

HRYUKIN
(coughing into his fist)
I was walking along here, not interfering with anyone, your honour. I was talking about firewood to Mitry Mitritch, when this low brute for no rhyme or reason bit my finger. I am a working man. Mine is fine work. I must have damages. {{8}}

OTCHUMYELOV
H'm. Very good. Whose dog is it? I won't let this pass! Yeldyrin, find out whose dog this is and draw up a report! And the dog must be strangled. Without delay! It's sure to be mad. {{9}}

SOMEONE IN THE CROWD
I fancy it's General Zhigalov's. {{10}}

OTCHUMYELOV
General Zhigalov's, h'm... Help me off with my coat, Yeldyrin... it's frightfully hot! {{11}}

Yeldyrin takes the overcoat. Otchumyelov turns on Hryukin.

OTCHUMYELOV (CONT'D)
It's a little dog, and you are a great hulking fellow! You must have scratched your finger with a nail, and then the idea struck you to get damages for it. I know you devils!

SOMEONE IN THE CROWD
He put a cigarette in her face, your honour, for a joke, and she had the sense to snap at him. {{12}}

HRYUKIN
That's a lie, Squinteye! You didn't see, so why tell lies about it? My own brother is in the gendarmes, let me tell you. {{13}}

OTCHUMYELOV
Don't argue! {{14}}

YELDYRIN
No, that's not the General's dog. The General hasn't got one like that. His are mostly setters. {{15}}

OTCHUMYELOV
I know it, too. The General has valuable dogs, thoroughbred, and this is goodness knows what! No coat, no shape. A low creature. You've been injured, Hryukin, and we can't let the matter drop. {{18}}

YELDYRIN
(thinking aloud)
Yet maybe it is the General's. It's not written on its face. I saw one like it the other day in his yard. {{19}}

VOICE IN THE CROWD
It is the General's, that's certain! {{20}}

OTCHUMYELOV
H'm, help me on with my overcoat, Yeldyrin, my lad... the wind's getting up. I am cold. You take it to the General's, and inquire there. A dog is a delicate animal. And you put your hand down, you blockhead. It's your own fault. {{21}}

Yeldyrin helps him back into the overcoat. The CROWD sniggers at Hryukin's finger.

{{22-23}}

PROHOR (60s), the General's cook, passes with a basket. Yeldyrin waves him over.

YELDYRIN
Hi, Prohor! Come here, my dear man! Look at this dog. Is it one of yours?

PROHOR
What an idea! We have never had one like that!

OTCHUMYELOV
There's no need to waste time asking. It's a stray dog! It must be destroyed, that's all about it. {{24}}

PROHOR
It is not our dog. It belongs to the General's brother, who arrived the other day. Our master does not care for hounds. But his honour is fond of them. {{25}}

OTCHUMYELOV
(beaming)
You don't say his Excellency's brother is here? Vladimir Ivanitch? Well, I never! Has he come on a visit? {{26}}

PROHOR
Yes. {{27}}

OTCHUMYELOV
Well, I never. He couldn't stay away from his brother. So this is his honour's dog? Delighted to hear it. Take it. It's not a bad pup. A lively creature. Snapped at this fellow's finger! Ha-ha-ha. Come, why are you shivering? Rrr... a nice little pup. {{28}}
